---
import Cite from '../../components/Cite.astro';
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import { codexPage } from '../../data/pages.js';

const page = codexPage('federations-and-guardians');
---
<Article slug={page.slug}>
  <p>
    A Fedimint federation is a small group of <strong>guardians</strong> who run a mint together. Users deposit bitcoin and get
    ecash notes of the same value. They can send those notes to each other, redeem them for bitcoin, or use them to pay Lightning
    invoices.<Cite page={page} s="fmHowItWorks" />
  </p>

  <h2>What guardians do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Each guardian runs a server. The servers agree on every transaction through consensus before any of them acts on it.<Cite page={page} s="fmArchitecture" /></li>
    <li>Together they hold the deposited bitcoin, and a threshold of them must sign to move it.</li>
    <li>They issue ecash with blind signatures, so they never learn which note belongs to whom.</li>
    <li>The federation keeps working while at most ⌊(n − 1)/3⌋ guardians are offline or misbehaving. Four guardians tolerate one.<Cite page={page} s="fmFederations" /></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who becomes a guardian</h2>
  <p>
    Guardians are usually people or organisations their users already know: a family, a club, a village co-operative. The founders
    choose them, then run a setup ceremony in which the guardians generate the federation’s keys together, so no one guardian ever
    holds a whole key.<Cite page={page} s="fmFounding" /><Cite page={page} s="fmGuardians" />
  </p>

  <h2>What you trust them with</h2>
  <p>
    A federation is a custodian. If enough guardians colluded, they could take the bitcoin they hold. What they cannot do is see your
    balance or link your payments, because the notes they sign are blinded.<Cite page={page} s="fmTrust" /> Spreading custody over
    several guardians means no single person can lose or steal the funds, but you still choose whom to trust.
  </p>

  <h2>How the game models it</h2>
  <p>
    Every player is a guardian of the Mint Citadel. Honest guardians finish tasks that mirror real guardian work, sign the withdrawals
    they can verify and refuse the rest. The match teaches the same lesson as a real federation: it is only as safe as the share of
    guardians who stay honest.
  </p>
</Article>
